{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789463789,"nonce":"DgxcD1qCcKKMXrt6BiQAzzFLzBI=","title":"Host state per stack: a Wren fiber switch under one task","message":"The world swaps an adapter's host state (Ash's trap chain and pending exception) around a task's turns, so a park in a Wren task keeps Haxe's traps to that task (df96108). Inside one task wren_lift switches stacks itself: `Fiber.call` into a fiber and `Fiber.yield` back, through krio, with no turn of the world. A Haxe try open on the calling fiber's stack is then the live trap chain while the called fiber runs, and a Haxe throw there lands on a frame of the other stack.\n\nThe seam already tells the adapter of every such switch, from the host side of it: `stack_suspended(outgoing, sp)` before the switch and `stack_suspended(target, sp)` after the return. What it does not say at the first call is which stack runs next. Design: a `stack_switch(from, to)` slot beside it (wren_lift knows both in `krio_call_once`), and host state per stack id in the core (`attach_stack_host_state`, swapped on the switch the adapter forwards), with the task's own state being the state of the stack the task runs on. Ash attaches a fresh exception state to a stack it first sees. The main context's stack is 0, as in the heap's registry.\n\nTest: a Wren task calls a fiber that calls Haxe code with a try, which calls back into Wren and yields; the task then calls Haxe code that throws and catches its own; then the first fiber is resumed and its Haxe frame throws and catches its own.","files":null}]}